      "documentConcept": "Bulk-write partial completion and ordering",
      "canonicalConceptId": "concept:94287ccec159c91632186f0839ac31e62a29379f721b1d0423d04f96a9feb240",
      "text": "Client bulk-write results retain indexed errors. The specification requires known individual success for `partialResult`; Node.js v7.6.0 attaches initialized results in the inspected error branches without that test, so object presence alone does not confirm successful completion. Node.js v7.6.0 source uses retryReads, the cursor session and same-address selection for eligible dual-label getMore overload retry; load-balanced dispatch reuses an existing session pin. Transaction, cursor-state, server-availability, attempt and CSOT guards constrain recovery. The bulk specification forbids ignoring killCursors results. The base command path checks and deserializes replies; cleanup discards the returned object and suppresses kill errors after possible session/server effects. Failed getMore preserves its original error through cleanup; bulk wrapping can retain its cause and partial result. Ordered-write iterator cleanup suppresses close rejection; initialization and normal close can surface helper failures. The bulk SHOULD exception, write-concern continuation, API-specific progress/transaction semantics, Atlas Serverless exclusion and MongoDB 8.1.2 mongos/sharded writeConcernError reporting also apply.",
